00:23:14.540 --> 00:23:16.830
much more familiar, I mean, I have walked,

00:23:16.830 --> 00:23:19.310
I could draw you a map of my neighborhood

00:23:19.310 --> 00:23:20.143
at this point.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:23.664
That doesn't actually
sound impressive, but okay.

00:23:24.653 --> 00:23:27.160
You couldn't draw a map of
your neighborhood before this?

00:23:27.160 --> 00:23:28.377
Okay, that's fine.

00:23:28.377 --> 00:23:29.210
That's, no judgment.

00:23:29.210 --> 00:23:30.365
No, I mean have you been,

00:23:30.365 --> 00:23:33.010
my neighborhood's got
a lot of weird streets

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:34.570
and there's no system.

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:35.960
There's not a grid.

00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:38.180
And so I'm just saying now I feel I...

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:40.560
I've been on streets that
are less than a mile away

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:41.907
that was like, "Never been on this street,

00:23:41.907 --> 00:23:42.740
"never been on this street."

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:45.570
I've been on all the
streets now, multiple times.

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:47.480
But I'm just saying that
the level of familiarity

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:51.030
with our family and our surroundings is at

00:23:51.030 --> 00:23:52.060
an all time high.

00:23:52.060 --> 00:23:53.282
Oh yeah.

00:23:53.282 --> 00:23:54.207
And it's creating some different dynamics.

00:23:54.207 --> 00:23:59.207
There was an article in the...

00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:01.850
Well, I've seen a few articles that talk

00:24:01.850 --> 00:24:03.400
about what's gonna happen?

00:24:03.400 --> 00:24:05.500
Is there gonna be a Coronavirus baby boom?

00:24:05.500 --> 00:24:07.490
Because all of these
couple are at home together

00:24:07.490 --> 00:24:08.780
and everybody's having sex,

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:09.927
like, "Ooh ah, it's just

00:24:09.927 --> 00:24:11.659
"a sex party all the time."
I know how babies are made.

00:24:11.659 --> 00:24:14.490
And then the other theory is

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:17.740
well actually there's gonna
be a boom in divorces.

00:24:17.740 --> 00:24:22.420
I think most likely you're
gonna see both of those things

00:24:22.420 --> 00:24:25.200
happening, hopefully
not in the same families

00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:26.720
together at the same time.

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:27.903
Hopefully they're happening
in separate families.

00:24:27.903 --> 00:24:31.230
I think there was the
honeymoon phase of quarantine

00:24:31.230 --> 00:24:36.230
that was like, "Hey, let's sort the mail,

00:24:36.637 --> 00:24:38.510
"this'll be fun."

00:24:38.510 --> 00:24:39.343
Right.

00:24:39.343 --> 00:24:42.607
And then a few weeks later it's like,

00:24:42.607 --> 00:24:44.207
"What are you?

00:24:44.207 --> 00:24:45.607
"We don't have any mail!"

00:24:47.793 --> 00:24:50.103
I checked the mailbox!

00:24:51.130 --> 00:24:53.520
And I walked straight from the mailbox

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:55.730
to the recycle bin because everything

00:24:55.730 --> 00:24:58.163
that was sent was junk!

00:24:59.540 --> 00:25:01.100
For those of you who may be confused

00:25:01.100 --> 00:25:02.360
or you're maybe you're a new listener

00:25:02.360 --> 00:25:04.140
to Ear Biscuits...

00:25:04.140 --> 00:25:04.973
We can have inside jokes.

00:25:04.973 --> 00:25:06.045
Don't explain it.

00:25:06.045 --> 00:25:08.900
I have to explain it because
I care about those people.

00:25:08.900 --> 00:25:12.380
Sorting the mail is a
euphemism, only on this podcast

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:14.750
for sexual intercourse.

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:16.960
I know how babies are made.

00:25:16.960 --> 00:25:19.910
Don't ask us how that
happened, but it did.

00:25:19.910 --> 00:25:21.527
It involved getting locked
I'm gonna review

00:25:21.527 --> 00:25:23.630
from an article...
in my bedroom, ironically.

00:25:23.630 --> 00:25:25.913
This is an article in the New York Post,

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:28.500
which is not typically a publication

00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:30.960
that I frequent,

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:35.393
but they had an article
about the surge in divorces.

00:25:36.320 --> 00:25:39.480
Copped up New Yorkers are
flooding lawyer phone lines

00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:41.950
with divorce inquiries,

00:25:41.950 --> 00:25:44.420
with an avalanche of filings expected

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:46.127
once the courts reopen.

00:25:46.127 --> 00:25:48.670
"People are realizing that
they can't stand each other,"

00:25:48.670 --> 00:25:51.980
said Manhattan lawyer,
Suzanne Kimberly Bracker,

00:25:51.980 --> 00:25:53.810
who like many in her
field has already seen

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:56.660
a Coronavirus divorce uptick.

00:25:56.660 --> 00:25:57.650
In the middle of the night,

00:25:57.650 --> 00:25:59.730
I got a call from a
client who now realizes

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:01.840
she has nothing in
common with her husband,

00:26:01.840 --> 00:26:05.120
but the children, and how he knows nothing

00:26:05.120 --> 00:26:06.070
about the children.

00:26:07.641 --> 00:26:08.880
So, it's like...

00:26:08.880 --> 00:26:11.970
And this is something that
again, this is sort of

00:26:11.970 --> 00:26:14.820
a stereotypical tropy thing,

00:26:14.820 --> 00:26:19.010
but you've got the dude
who's been off at his job

00:26:19.010 --> 00:26:22.127
and then he comes back
home and he is like,

00:26:22.127 --> 00:26:25.647
"Oh, now I've got to engage with the kids

00:26:25.647 --> 00:26:27.337
"more than just like hey, I'm home,

00:26:27.337 --> 00:26:29.337
"Dad's home, let's shoot some basketball

00:26:29.337 --> 00:26:31.810
"for 30 minutes and that
makes me a great dad."

00:26:31.810 --> 00:26:33.360
But you know what I'm saying?
Yeah.

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:35.630
Now you're expected,

00:26:35.630 --> 00:26:40.630
and a lot of people are
continuing to do their jobs

00:26:41.070 --> 00:26:43.830
from home, which can be
a little bit disruptive.

00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:45.470
I mean, even right now, we're sitting here

00:26:45.470 --> 00:26:46.610
in the middle of our homes

00:26:46.610 --> 00:26:48.810
making everybody else be relatively quiet

00:26:48.810 --> 00:26:49.760
while we do this.

00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:53.260
Yeah, typically Lily will roll out of bed

00:26:53.260 --> 00:26:54.500
and she'll walk in here

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:57.400
and she'll see me doing this

00:26:57.400 --> 00:27:01.610
and then she will just
slump over, roll her eyes,

00:27:01.610 --> 00:27:02.650
and go back into her bedroom

00:27:02.650 --> 00:27:06.230
because she realizes
she can't make breakfast

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:08.900
because it makes too much noise.

00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:11.080
And I told her she could
tiptoe around and make

00:27:11.080 --> 00:27:13.710
the breakfast, but you know what?

00:27:13.710 --> 00:27:15.060
She's being ultrasensitive.

00:27:16.570 --> 00:27:18.640
I mean, not sensitive, but considerate

00:27:18.640 --> 00:27:19.860
and she's not doing it.

00:27:19.860 --> 00:27:23.440
But it's like yeah, so
our work lives coming home

00:27:23.440 --> 00:27:26.830
is a severe inconvenience to our families.

00:27:26.830 --> 00:27:28.470
You're in the freaking
middle of your living room.

00:27:28.470 --> 00:27:30.660
If somebody wanted to
watch TV this morning,

00:27:30.660 --> 00:27:31.493
that ain't happening--

00:27:31.493 --> 00:27:32.710
There's other televisions.

00:27:32.710 --> 00:27:36.400
Well, it wouldn't be on
the big one on your left.

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:37.592
Exactly.

00:27:37.592 --> 00:27:41.250
Yeah and one of the things
that's happening too

00:27:41.250 --> 00:27:43.890
is I think that a lot of people are seeing

00:27:43.890 --> 00:27:47.760
their working parent
or their working spouse

00:27:47.760 --> 00:27:49.985
in work mode in a way that they haven't.

00:27:49.985 --> 00:27:51.943
This is in an article
from the Washington Post.

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:55.130
This woman, Laura Norkin,

00:27:55.130 --> 00:27:57.935
Deputy Editor of In-Style Magazine,

00:27:57.935 --> 00:28:01.620
tweeted the funny thing about quarantining

00:28:01.620 --> 00:28:03.700
is hearing your partner in full work mode

00:28:03.700 --> 00:28:05.446
for the first time.

00:28:05.446 --> 00:28:06.279
Mm, mm hmm.

00:28:06.279 --> 00:28:09.263
Like I'm married to a let's
circle back guy, who knew?

00:28:10.522 --> 00:28:13.409
It's like let's circle back.
It's like I've never heard

00:28:13.409 --> 00:28:16.840
somebody use that.
We can take this offline.

00:28:16.840 --> 00:28:19.140
And then circle back on it later

00:28:19.140 --> 00:28:21.740
and then we can close the loop

00:28:21.740 --> 00:28:24.280
when we've taken the pin out of it,

00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:26.340
but for now we're putting the pin in it.

00:28:26.340 --> 00:28:27.381
Put a pin in it.

00:28:27.381 --> 00:28:30.550
Now I will say there are
some positives from this,

00:28:30.550 --> 00:28:34.570
so as you know we have
our company wide meeting

00:28:34.570 --> 00:28:36.460
and there's not really a
place in my house to get away

00:28:36.460 --> 00:28:40.730
even when you go into the
most private of places,

00:28:40.730 --> 00:28:43.102
which would be our little guest bedroom.

00:28:43.102 --> 00:28:45.900
The crack under the door is like an inch,

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:48.510
so you can hear everything
that's happening in there.

00:28:48.510 --> 00:28:51.180
And Jessie was working
outside on her computer

00:28:51.180 --> 00:28:53.480
right outside the room
and we had our meeting,

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:56.250
we have it every two weeks
where everybody in Mythical

00:28:56.250 --> 00:28:59.610
gets on a video chat and
we talk about business

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:02.616
and also just connect with one another.

00:29:02.616 --> 00:29:05.473
And I come out of the
door and she was like,

00:29:07.977 --> 00:29:09.887
"You sound like a good boss."

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:12.143
That's nice.

00:29:12.143 --> 00:29:13.347
You sound like a good boss.

00:29:13.347 --> 00:29:17.017
She's like, "You sound
like a nice, nice boss."

00:29:17.017 --> 00:29:18.190
Was she surprised?

00:29:18.190 --> 00:29:20.670
And I was like, "Oh, thank you."

00:29:20.670 --> 00:29:23.467
I think that she knows that I'm not,

00:29:23.467 --> 00:29:28.467
neither of us are those typical
chew your head off bosses,

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:31.493
that's just not in our
character, disposition.

00:29:31.493 --> 00:29:34.300
But I think listening in on the meeting

00:29:34.300 --> 00:29:36.413
and again just hearing my side of it.

00:29:37.699 --> 00:29:40.587
So I didn't come out and she said,

00:29:40.587 --> 00:29:41.710
"Oh, you're a circle back guy,"

00:29:41.710 --> 00:29:42.930
she said, "Oh, you're a nice boss."

00:29:42.930 --> 00:29:44.090
I was like, "Well, good, yeah."

00:29:44.090 --> 00:29:46.610
I'm gonna get a mug that
says I'm a nice boss.

00:29:46.610 --> 00:29:49.410
As I'm setting things up
for Good Mythical Morning

00:29:49.410 --> 00:29:54.057
or for this show, I
notice Christy was home,

00:29:54.057 --> 00:29:56.420
she'll like look at me

00:29:56.420 --> 00:29:58.654
and like she's assessing me.

00:29:58.654 --> 00:30:00.810
Even just right before we start recording,

00:30:00.810 --> 00:30:02.310
she comes right over the camera,

00:30:02.310 --> 00:30:04.320
she pokes her head
around from the kitchen.

00:30:04.320 --> 00:30:07.180
She's like, "Are you okay?"

00:30:07.180 --> 00:30:08.520
Now that's a loaded question.

00:30:08.520 --> 00:30:09.353
There's a little more to it

00:30:09.353 --> 00:30:10.610
that I could get into it a little bit,

00:30:10.610 --> 00:30:12.410
but I'm like, "I'm fine."

00:30:12.410 --> 00:30:13.500
She's like, "You look pale."

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:15.510
I'm like, what I'm thinking is,

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:18.280
I get stressed out right
before I do something,

00:30:18.280 --> 00:30:19.890
especially when there's all these people

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:22.020
and groceries came in this morning again,

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:23.258
oh, good Lord.

00:30:23.258 --> 00:30:24.613
Oh yeah, that'll throw you off.

00:30:24.613 --> 00:30:25.446
Don't get me going.

00:30:25.446 --> 00:30:26.279
I'm like yeah I'm pale.

00:30:26.279 --> 00:30:27.300
I get pale before I start anything

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:30.600
because I'm frigging stress spiking

00:30:31.730 --> 00:30:33.650
and then I get my robe on and I sit down

00:30:33.650 --> 00:30:34.483
and I'm like,

00:30:35.388 --> 00:30:36.950
"All right, all right."

00:30:36.950 --> 00:30:38.660
And then I mellow out a little bit

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:40.180
and then I get in the zone.

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:41.610
The auto zone.

00:30:41.610 --> 00:30:43.174
But she's like,

00:30:43.174 --> 00:30:45.630
"You are so worked up."
Not a sponsor.

00:30:45.630 --> 00:30:47.850
I'm like, "Yeah, I get worked up."

00:30:47.850 --> 00:30:49.400
That's why it's called work.

00:30:49.400 --> 00:30:50.503
I get up for it.

00:30:54.684 --> 00:30:55.517
So I didn't get it.

00:30:55.517 --> 00:30:58.194
You're a good boss, but she's
not listening in on our--

00:30:58.194 --> 00:31:00.409
Oh, I've gotten other things too.

00:31:00.409 --> 00:31:01.505
In on our "all hands" meeting

00:31:01.505 --> 00:31:03.160
as we used to call it at IBM.

