You're watching Survivor every night?

00:34:32.670 --> 00:34:33.890
Every single night

00:34:33.890 --> 00:34:35.540
and it's too much.

00:34:35.540 --> 00:34:37.223
It's a problem.
Yeah.

00:34:37.223 --> 00:34:40.500
And then we're not having
enough other positive outlets

00:34:40.500 --> 00:34:42.520
as a family, so I'm doing the AMA and

00:34:42.520 --> 00:34:45.230
their outside playing ping pong and then,

00:34:45.230 --> 00:34:47.500
this is not the first nor the second time

00:34:47.500 --> 00:34:49.000
this has happened in the past three days,

00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:50.537
but all of the sudden,

00:34:50.537 --> 00:34:53.090
I hear Lando screaming.

00:34:53.090 --> 00:34:54.320
And a lot of times when he's screaming,

00:34:54.320 --> 00:34:56.503
he's screaming, "Delete it!

00:34:57.516 --> 00:34:59.370
"Delete it!."

00:35:00.230 --> 00:35:01.063
What?

00:35:01.063 --> 00:35:02.410
"Delete it!"

00:35:02.410 --> 00:35:05.407
And he comes in here and he
runs to me and he's like,

00:35:05.407 --> 00:35:10.277
"Dad, Lincoln took video of
me and he won't delete it."

00:35:10.277 --> 00:35:11.297
I'm like, "What's he gonna do?

00:35:11.297 --> 00:35:12.490
"Is he gonna post it online?"

00:35:12.490 --> 00:35:13.340
He's like, "He might."

00:35:13.340 --> 00:35:14.450
I was like, "Has he ever done that?"

00:35:14.450 --> 00:35:17.980
Then Lincoln walks in and
he has this Lincoln grin

00:35:17.980 --> 00:35:19.200
on his face.

00:35:19.200 --> 00:35:20.680
He thinks...

00:35:20.680 --> 00:35:23.803
He is enjoying this moment.

00:35:25.010 --> 00:35:27.425
This is what he's doing it for.

00:35:27.425 --> 00:35:28.258
Yeah.

00:35:28.258 --> 00:35:29.091
He's a big brother.
To drive him crazy.

00:35:29.091 --> 00:35:33.930
You would of thought that
Lincoln filmed Lando confessing

00:35:33.930 --> 00:35:34.793
to a murder.

00:35:35.830 --> 00:35:38.913
It's like that's the level of intensity.

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:43.280
And then the more that
Lincoln just grins at him,

00:35:43.280 --> 00:35:45.640
the more he just goes ballistic.

00:35:45.640 --> 00:35:48.290
And I get to a point where I'm like...

00:35:49.370 --> 00:35:53.250
And then three hours later, I'm queuing

00:35:53.250 --> 00:35:55.310
up Survivor, okay, finally we can have

00:35:55.310 --> 00:35:56.840
our positive time together.

00:35:56.840 --> 00:35:57.850
We can agree on this,

00:35:57.850 --> 00:35:59.900
we can at least be in the same room,

00:35:59.900 --> 00:36:01.843
but not at each other's throats.

00:36:03.320 --> 00:36:05.970
And I'm like, they wouldn't
come and they wouldn't come,

00:36:05.970 --> 00:36:08.690
so I've got this technique
where I start playing Survivor

00:36:08.690 --> 00:36:09.523
really loud.

00:36:09.523 --> 00:36:11.480
And then they'll stop
what they're doing and

00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:13.150
they'll all run in, they'll
get off their phones

00:36:13.150 --> 00:36:14.300
or whatever and run in.

00:36:15.430 --> 00:36:18.220
Well this time, instead of that happening,

00:36:18.220 --> 00:36:20.028
it was, "Delete it!

00:36:20.028 --> 00:36:21.028
"Delete it!"

00:36:22.520 --> 00:36:24.730
And they ran in and you know what?

00:36:24.730 --> 00:36:25.630
Without thinking about it,

00:36:25.630 --> 00:36:28.942
I was like, "I don't wanna hear it!

00:36:28.942 --> 00:36:33.942
"You two go back out of
the room and handle it!"

00:36:36.310 --> 00:36:37.640
And then they stood there,

00:36:37.640 --> 00:36:39.940
kinda like bumfuzzled,

00:36:39.940 --> 00:36:42.480
and I was like, "Go out of the room

00:36:43.550 --> 00:36:44.883
"and handle it!"

00:36:46.229 --> 00:36:47.130
And I looked at Christy,

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:50.480
who I was talking with
before they came in and

00:36:50.480 --> 00:36:52.830
she was like, she had
this disappointed look

00:36:52.830 --> 00:36:53.663
on her face

00:36:55.180 --> 00:36:57.213
at me.
Yeah, yeah.

00:36:58.470 --> 00:37:02.507
And then the boys left and I'm like,

00:37:02.507 --> 00:37:03.657
"What were you saying?"

00:37:07.818 --> 00:37:10.550
It's like at a certain point

00:37:11.446 --> 00:37:15.560
the button's been pushed so much

00:37:15.560 --> 00:37:18.330
that it won't pop back up.

00:37:18.330 --> 00:37:19.163
You know that?

00:37:19.163 --> 00:37:20.440
You know that type of button?

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:23.050
Where it gets pushed in
and then it gets lodged

00:37:23.050 --> 00:37:24.670
underneath something, so it's like...

00:37:24.670 --> 00:37:26.500
Or you think that the
button popped back up,

00:37:26.500 --> 00:37:29.050
but really like you push it and

00:37:29.050 --> 00:37:30.720
instead of it popping back up,

00:37:30.720 --> 00:37:32.280
you push it and then you watch it,

00:37:32.280 --> 00:37:34.425
I don't think the button...

00:37:34.425 --> 00:37:36.444
And then all of the sudden it starts

00:37:36.444 --> 00:37:37.280
to come back up a little
bit and then stops.

00:37:37.280 --> 00:37:39.570
That happens with literally
every keyboard that

00:37:39.570 --> 00:37:41.668
we put in our kitchen...

00:37:41.668 --> 00:37:42.501
Jelly.

00:37:42.501 --> 00:37:44.200
Because no matter how
many times I tell my kids

00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:46.220
to wash their hands before they type,

00:37:46.220 --> 00:37:48.020
they don't, they won't.

00:37:48.020 --> 00:37:50.280
So we go through one magic Apple keyboard

00:37:50.280 --> 00:37:51.640
every seven months.

00:37:51.640 --> 00:37:54.063
You talk about being a good boss?

00:37:54.063 --> 00:37:54.900
It's part of my budget.

00:37:54.900 --> 00:37:57.360
And the way that we talk to our employees,

00:37:57.360 --> 00:37:59.560
sometimes it gets familial.

00:37:59.560 --> 00:38:01.650
There's a good level of family atmosphere

00:38:01.650 --> 00:38:03.250
and team atmosphere,

00:38:03.250 --> 00:38:05.130
but if I talk...

00:38:06.060 --> 00:38:08.032
Man, I'm just being honest.

00:38:08.032 --> 00:38:12.012
You'd be shocked if you saw

00:38:12.012 --> 00:38:17.012
some of the things that I exude,

00:38:17.890 --> 00:38:18.723
especially in this time-.

00:38:18.723 --> 00:38:19.801
Well, okay.

00:38:19.801 --> 00:38:21.830
And to support you in this
I've already said

00:38:21.830 --> 00:38:23.100
that I've squeezed my kids,

00:38:23.100 --> 00:38:25.150
but that was a joke.

00:38:25.150 --> 00:38:29.690
Well, but in terms of the
losing it and yelling...

00:38:31.473 --> 00:38:34.990
And first of all there's
people who are in families

00:38:34.990 --> 00:38:36.673
where that doesn't ever happen.

00:38:37.548 --> 00:38:38.470
Right.

00:38:38.470 --> 00:38:40.981
And it's great for you.

00:38:40.981 --> 00:38:45.737
And also you might be
shocked when you hear,

00:38:45.737 --> 00:38:48.120
"Oh, you guys yell at your children?"

00:38:48.120 --> 00:38:51.190
'Cuz I know, I know that
my kids have friends

00:38:51.190 --> 00:38:53.070
whose parents never yell at them

00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:54.830
and that's just not our family.

00:38:54.830 --> 00:38:58.873
I'm not proud of it,
it's just the way we are.

00:39:00.790 --> 00:39:05.133
And like Jessie will be
working with Shepard,

00:39:06.530 --> 00:39:07.500
getting him to do...

00:39:07.500 --> 00:39:10.790
Now again, she's homeschooled
the kids for forever

00:39:10.790 --> 00:39:14.940
and they've only been in
school for three years now,

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:16.860
but of course she thought she was done

00:39:17.710 --> 00:39:21.820
and now everybody's homeschooling, right?

00:39:21.820 --> 00:39:24.068
And they're doing the
distance learning thing, but

00:39:24.068 --> 00:39:25.810
with Shepard, especially,

00:39:25.810 --> 00:39:28.220
that doesn't mean that he's
just gonna do the work.

00:39:28.220 --> 00:39:31.140
You've gotta stay with him
and make him do the work,

00:39:31.140 --> 00:39:32.690
keep him from being distracted.

00:39:33.670 --> 00:39:38.670
And Jessie is more
patient than me and tries

00:39:39.310 --> 00:39:43.328
to engage with him in a fruitful way,

00:39:43.328 --> 00:39:45.090
in an effective way.
Yeah, we both married

00:39:45.090 --> 00:39:47.130
much better people than we are.

00:39:47.130 --> 00:39:49.820
And that's a wonderful thing for us.

00:39:49.820 --> 00:39:50.750
And then she will...

00:39:50.750 --> 00:39:54.480
But then I'll come out
of a meeting or podcast,

00:39:54.480 --> 00:39:56.053
or whatever I happen to be doing and

00:39:56.053 --> 00:39:59.490
she'll say, "Your turn."

00:39:59.490 --> 00:40:02.300
And this is not something
that happens when I'm at work,

00:40:02.300 --> 00:40:04.230
but when I'm at home
working, it does happen

00:40:04.230 --> 00:40:05.780
on a regular basis.

00:40:05.780 --> 00:40:08.540
And then I'll go in there and I'll enter

00:40:08.540 --> 00:40:10.290
into the situation with Shepard

00:40:11.680 --> 00:40:14.110
with some patience at the beginning

00:40:14.110 --> 00:40:15.610
that is exhausted...

00:40:15.610 --> 00:40:19.470
I think my patience tank
needs to be repaired,

00:40:19.470 --> 00:40:21.320
it has several leaks.

00:40:21.320 --> 00:40:24.050
And as soon as I fill it
up, Shepard finds a way

00:40:24.050 --> 00:40:27.790
to pull the plugs out

00:40:27.790 --> 00:40:32.620
and basically I've gone
from trying to work

00:40:32.620 --> 00:40:35.630
with him to just telling him...

00:40:36.500 --> 00:40:38.890
Just basically shaming him.

00:40:38.890 --> 00:40:41.603
That's ultimately what
yelling at somebody does.

00:40:41.603 --> 00:40:44.057
You're trying to use shame
to get them to do it.

00:40:44.057 --> 00:40:45.770
And I know it's not effective.

00:40:45.770 --> 00:40:48.160
When I'm doing it, I
know it's not effective,

00:40:48.160 --> 00:40:50.560
but that's what I end up resorting to

00:40:50.560 --> 00:40:52.407
very quickly--
In my mind...

00:40:52.407 --> 00:40:54.728
And your tank gets smaller
and smaller and smaller

00:40:54.728 --> 00:40:57.123
the more time that you spend together.

00:40:58.119 --> 00:41:00.020
In my mind I'm thinking and I did once

00:41:00.020 --> 00:41:02.680
I did tell Christy at that point,

00:41:02.680 --> 00:41:06.760
I was like, I figured
this was worth a shot.

00:41:06.760 --> 00:41:09.937
After I said, "Just resolve it yourself,

00:41:09.937 --> 00:41:11.220
"handle it!."

00:41:11.220 --> 00:41:12.617
And they left the room and I was like

00:41:12.617 --> 00:41:13.460
"What were we talking about?"

00:41:13.460 --> 00:41:18.460
The I was like I yelled
in a calculated way

00:41:19.160 --> 00:41:22.370
because I want

00:41:22.370 --> 00:41:26.200
'cuz we don't yell that often.

00:41:26.200 --> 00:41:29.144
I mean it's not something,
I mean there are

00:41:29.144 --> 00:41:33.780
family cultures where little
subcultures of families

00:41:33.780 --> 00:41:37.060
where they yell more and it's
just like everybody's got

00:41:37.060 --> 00:41:38.990
a thick skin when it
comes to that and it's

00:41:38.990 --> 00:41:41.630
a form of communication, everybody knows

00:41:41.630 --> 00:41:46.630
how to handle it and I
don't think it's absolutely

00:41:47.070 --> 00:41:49.398
a horrible thing to raise your voice.

00:41:49.398 --> 00:41:54.398
I would imagine that there's home climates

00:41:54.810 --> 00:41:57.960
where it can just be,

00:41:57.960 --> 00:41:59.710
you can be expressive,

00:41:59.710 --> 00:42:02.840
but it very quickly
goes into a danger zone

00:42:03.740 --> 00:42:07.120
and that's what we feel
in our house and so

00:42:07.120 --> 00:42:09.900
the goal is to always
remain in control and not

00:42:09.900 --> 00:42:10.990
to raise your voice.

00:42:10.990 --> 00:42:13.070
I mean, we all agree on that over here.

00:42:13.070 --> 00:42:14.910
And that's...

00:42:14.910 --> 00:42:16.690
I wouldn't put that
standard on anybody else

00:42:16.690 --> 00:42:19.730
'cuz I'm not a psychologist,

00:42:19.730 --> 00:42:21.810
but for me, that's where we've landed.

00:42:21.810 --> 00:42:24.080
So it does make me feel like a failure

00:42:24.080 --> 00:42:25.753
when I do yell,

00:42:28.067 --> 00:42:30.580
but I thought it was worth a shot

00:42:30.580 --> 00:42:33.300
for me to say, "This
is a calculated yell,"

00:42:33.300 --> 00:42:38.300
for me to express how
much of an impact this

00:42:39.245 --> 00:42:44.245
ongoing quarrel has on me.

00:42:44.426 --> 00:42:48.227
It's like, "Oh, dad must
feel strongly about this.

00:42:48.227 --> 00:42:50.760
"This is impacting him."

00:42:50.760 --> 00:42:51.593
Yeah.

00:42:51.593 --> 00:42:53.440
I mean, it wasn't that
compelling of an argument,

00:42:53.440 --> 00:42:56.336
but I tried.
Did it work?

00:42:56.336 --> 00:42:57.376
No, it wasn't that Christy was mad at me.

00:42:57.376 --> 00:42:59.910
I mean, we just, she
helped me talk through it

00:42:59.910 --> 00:43:01.460
and then I was like--

00:43:01.460 --> 00:43:05.283
No, did they resolve their
conflict and come back in?

00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:08.680
Yeah, well, I didn't hear it anymore.

00:43:08.680 --> 00:43:09.860
They shut up.

00:43:09.860 --> 00:43:12.060
Which again, that was my goal.

00:43:12.060 --> 00:43:14.670
It was like stop impacting
my quality of life.

00:43:14.670 --> 00:43:18.370
It was that selfish
stance of I don't care,

00:43:18.370 --> 00:43:20.680
as long as you guys can
shut up somewhere else

00:43:20.680 --> 00:43:23.780
and I don't hear it, I will be fine.

00:43:23.780 --> 00:43:28.780
But then later on we called
them back in there and

00:43:29.450 --> 00:43:32.837
we had a 10 minute discussion
that was more like,

00:43:32.837 --> 00:43:36.340
"Hey, how do we get to
the bottom of this?"

00:43:36.340 --> 00:43:39.680
Now, in retrospect, I might,
could've added an apology

00:43:39.680 --> 00:43:43.683
about losing my cool and raising my voice,

00:43:44.820 --> 00:43:46.490
especially when you make
the point about shaming

00:43:46.490 --> 00:43:49.030
because I think that's a good point.

00:43:49.030 --> 00:43:50.270
Maybe I'll revisit that.

00:43:50.270 --> 00:43:52.840
But we at least had a
constructive conversation

00:43:52.840 --> 00:43:56.390
about how they can own
what they're doing and

00:43:56.390 --> 00:43:59.690
what their motives are and...

00:43:59.690 --> 00:44:01.160
'Cuz Lincoln's doing it on purpose

00:44:01.160 --> 00:44:03.600
and now Lando's developing
this coping mechanism

00:44:03.600 --> 00:44:05.990
of yelling at him, and
this is a new thing.

00:44:05.990 --> 00:44:07.890
So it's like trying to
get to the bottom of that.

00:44:07.890 --> 00:44:12.010
But I mean and this came
at the end of a long day.

00:44:12.010 --> 00:44:14.834
I mean, at the beginning of the day,

00:44:14.834 --> 00:44:16.760
in a different way than Shepard,

00:44:16.760 --> 00:44:20.150
but we're having, we're
just trying to get Lincoln

00:44:20.150 --> 00:44:21.760
to be more motivated to do his schoolwork.

00:44:21.760 --> 00:44:25.070
It's very difficult to do schoolwork

00:44:25.070 --> 00:44:29.210
in this environment and
he comes in this morning

00:44:29.210 --> 00:44:32.710
and he's like, "Mom, dad,
I've done some research."

00:44:32.710 --> 00:44:34.220
And I'm like, I perk up,

00:44:34.220 --> 00:44:36.963
I'm like my boy's done research.

00:44:38.408 --> 00:44:39.241
This is great.

00:44:39.241 --> 00:44:40.310
I immediately felt proud.

00:44:40.310 --> 00:44:43.250
I was like I've never heard
him use that phrase before.

00:44:43.250 --> 00:44:45.313
I've done some research.

00:44:46.370 --> 00:44:50.967
And he says, "The LA
Unified School District

00:44:50.967 --> 00:44:55.467
"says that the grades
that you had when you left

00:44:55.467 --> 00:44:58.467
"for quarantine, they
can't make your grade lower

00:44:58.467 --> 00:45:02.197
"than that, so I'm not gonna
do anymore schoolwork."

00:45:03.290 --> 00:45:04.943
I was like well this took a turn.

00:45:06.100 --> 00:45:09.820
And then Christy was
like, "Who told you this?"

00:45:09.820 --> 00:45:12.090
Yeah, that's some high school rumor, man.

00:45:12.090 --> 00:45:14.570
Well my friends aren't doing their work

00:45:14.570 --> 00:45:16.020
'cuz they said that's true.

00:45:16.020 --> 00:45:18.180
And she's like first of all,

00:45:18.180 --> 00:45:20.463
we're not in the LA
Unified School District,

00:45:21.890 --> 00:45:24.200
so you need to do more research.

00:45:24.200 --> 00:45:26.610
And so we on and off all day

00:45:26.610 --> 00:45:28.327
we were bantering, getting into that

00:45:28.327 --> 00:45:32.160
and then Lando and him are
at each other's throats

00:45:32.160 --> 00:45:34.540
because I won't let them
be on screens all day

00:45:34.540 --> 00:45:37.320
and I force them to go
outside and play together

00:45:37.320 --> 00:45:40.393
and then they come in
at each other's throats.

00:45:41.905 --> 00:45:46.220
And Lily had her own...

00:45:46.220 --> 00:45:48.440
I'm not gonna tell Lily's story on here,

00:45:48.440 --> 00:45:53.440
but it was something that,
Lily suffered a disappointment,

00:45:53.700 --> 00:45:56.080
like she got some disappointing news,

00:45:56.080 --> 00:45:58.590
something she was looking forward to,

00:45:58.590 --> 00:46:00.550
it went the other way.

00:46:00.550 --> 00:46:04.290
And this was like months
of looking forward

00:46:04.290 --> 00:46:05.773
to getting this news.

00:46:08.550 --> 00:46:11.570
And so it was a bit shattering

00:46:13.182 --> 00:46:16.310
for Christy and I, as well as for her.

00:46:16.310 --> 00:46:19.063
So it's like that was also yesterday.

00:46:24.035 --> 00:46:29.035
You have these ideas that things are...

00:46:29.037 --> 00:46:32.030
And on previous podcasts
we talk about this mindset

00:46:32.030 --> 00:46:33.720
of seeing things as an opportunity

00:46:33.720 --> 00:46:38.440
and not being Pollyanna
about how things are going

00:46:38.440 --> 00:46:40.500
in the world or even in our homes,

00:46:40.500 --> 00:46:43.980
but feeling like, okay, you know what?

00:46:43.980 --> 00:46:45.300
We can find the silver lining,

00:46:45.300 --> 00:46:47.834
we can see the opportunities in this,

00:46:47.834 --> 00:46:50.267
we can like the things
that you listed out.

00:46:50.267 --> 00:46:52.640
And then knowing at the end of this

00:46:52.640 --> 00:46:57.640
that we're going to see
it as a precious time

00:46:58.160 --> 00:47:01.409
because we do love each
other and thank goodness

00:47:01.409 --> 00:47:05.078
we care about each other and we're not...

00:47:05.078 --> 00:47:07.982
There's a lot of people
who a) don't have anybody

00:47:07.982 --> 00:47:11.050
or they're on the outs of a relationship

00:47:11.050 --> 00:47:12.242
or they were about to break up.

00:47:12.242 --> 00:47:15.420
There's lots of more nightmare situations

00:47:15.420 --> 00:47:16.560
than the ones we're in.

00:47:16.560 --> 00:47:19.213
We've got it really
great with our families.

00:47:21.510 --> 00:47:24.603
But it just gets to you, man.

00:47:26.100 --> 00:47:27.789
Well and I think that it's,

00:47:27.789 --> 00:47:29.850
I mean, I do think that it's important

00:47:31.440 --> 00:47:34.920
to recognize that because I'm
sure you could say something,

00:47:34.920 --> 00:47:37.570
and I'm sure someone has
written something like this

00:47:37.570 --> 00:47:41.600
where it's like it's
not that being cooped up

00:47:41.600 --> 00:47:46.600
in your home is going to cause a divorce,

00:47:46.620 --> 00:47:49.350
it's that being cooped
up in your home is going

00:47:49.350 --> 00:47:54.350
to reveal the reality of whether or not

00:47:54.440 --> 00:47:55.630
you actually connect.

00:47:55.630 --> 00:47:58.540
That's the kind of BS
that somebody would say

00:47:58.540 --> 00:48:01.330
because the fact is is that
regardless of the nature

00:48:01.330 --> 00:48:05.083
of your relationship,
regardless of how healthy it is,

00:48:06.920 --> 00:48:09.830
this is going to be
difficult for almost everyone

00:48:09.830 --> 00:48:14.430
and that doesn't mean
that you should break up

00:48:14.430 --> 00:48:17.100
or you should get a divorce,
or you're not compatible.

00:48:17.100 --> 00:48:21.069
Now, that also doesn't mean that

00:48:21.069 --> 00:48:24.360
there will be some real
discovery and real discussion

00:48:24.360 --> 00:48:26.810
during this time that you
realize you got some things

00:48:26.810 --> 00:48:28.690
to work on or maybe you do realize that

00:48:28.690 --> 00:48:31.550
you've come to an impasse
that you can't get through,

00:48:31.550 --> 00:48:34.647
but I think it's helpful that you're,

00:48:34.647 --> 00:48:39.647
that it's incredibly normal to respond

00:48:39.780 --> 00:48:42.931
to this situation in a negative way

00:48:42.931 --> 00:48:47.695
and have to resist being selfish
and being self interested,

00:48:47.695 --> 00:48:50.290
and also think there is some,

00:48:50.290 --> 00:48:51.470
depending on your personality,

00:48:51.470 --> 00:48:53.580
I mean, for me, as an introvert, it's like

00:48:54.690 --> 00:48:56.460
it's not a negative thing

00:48:56.460 --> 00:48:59.193
when I think, "I gotta get
away from these people."

00:48:59.193 --> 00:49:01.320
That doesn't make me a bad person,

00:49:01.320 --> 00:49:03.130
it doesn't make them bad people,

00:49:03.130 --> 00:49:06.060
it just means that the Rhett-ster needs

00:49:06.060 --> 00:49:09.197
to ejector himself from this situation

00:49:09.197 --> 00:49:11.410
Right.
And go on a solo walk.

00:49:11.410 --> 00:49:13.120
In fact, Jessie and I were
talking about this yesterday

00:49:13.120 --> 00:49:15.960
because Jessie is a...

00:49:15.960 --> 00:49:17.670
One of the biggest differences
between the two of us,

00:49:17.670 --> 00:49:18.750
we have so many things in common,

00:49:18.750 --> 00:49:19.583
we enjoy so many things,

00:49:19.583 --> 00:49:21.850
we connect in a lot of different ways,

00:49:21.850 --> 00:49:23.550
but she is a hyper-extrovert

00:49:23.550 --> 00:49:26.020
and I'm not a hyper-introvert,

00:49:26.020 --> 00:49:28.180
I present as an extrovert to most people

00:49:28.180 --> 00:49:29.440
who get to know me for the first time,

00:49:29.440 --> 00:49:31.860
but in reality I'm
energized by being alone,

00:49:31.860 --> 00:49:33.110
so I'm an introvert.

00:49:33.110 --> 00:49:37.850
But, so when I talk about things like I'm

00:49:37.850 --> 00:49:38.760
gonna go work out.

00:49:38.760 --> 00:49:41.950
The first week she was like,
"You wanna work out together?"

00:49:41.950 --> 00:49:43.519
And I'm like, "No!

00:49:43.519 --> 00:49:45.317
"I don't wanna work out together."

00:49:46.646 --> 00:49:48.094
For like seven reasons,
the least of which being

00:49:48.094 --> 00:49:49.820
that we're not gonna do the same stuff

00:49:49.820 --> 00:49:52.820
in the workout, but the main reason being

00:49:54.260 --> 00:49:57.410
that it's a time for me.

00:49:57.410 --> 00:50:00.706
I put on, I listen to a book.

00:50:00.706 --> 00:50:03.390
I don't listen to pump
up music when I work out,

00:50:03.390 --> 00:50:04.810
I listen to books on tape

00:50:04.810 --> 00:50:07.562
and when I walk around the
neighborhood by myself,

00:50:07.562 --> 00:50:09.007
today, I listen as well,

00:50:09.007 --> 00:50:10.771
but it's like--

00:50:10.771 --> 00:50:11.700
You got a Walkman?

00:50:11.700 --> 00:50:13.486
A cassette tape?

00:50:13.486 --> 00:50:17.740
No, I just have, I've just
got earphones and an iPhone.

00:50:17.740 --> 00:50:21.673
Yeah it's your time and
you need that recharging.

00:50:21.673 --> 00:50:22.506
And I need it.

00:50:22.506 --> 00:50:26.810
Even today or yesterday when...

00:50:26.810 --> 00:50:31.810
Our days right now
consist of doing something

00:50:32.250 --> 00:50:33.610
in the morning and
something in the afternoon

00:50:33.610 --> 00:50:35.250
that's work related typically, right,

00:50:35.250 --> 00:50:36.820
every weekday?

00:50:36.820 --> 00:50:39.030
And even something as simple as like,

00:50:39.030 --> 00:50:41.630
okay, let's record two episodes

00:50:41.630 --> 00:50:42.820
of Good Mythical Morning today.

00:50:42.820 --> 00:50:44.960
Like there's been some days
where we've recorded one

00:50:44.960 --> 00:50:46.850
in the morning and one in the evening,

00:50:46.850 --> 00:50:48.000
or one in the afternoon

00:50:49.320 --> 00:50:50.540
and you would think

00:50:50.540 --> 00:50:52.363
oh, two episodes of GMM, that's,

00:50:52.363 --> 00:50:54.595
well what else have you got going on?

00:50:54.595 --> 00:50:55.428
That's an easy day.

00:50:55.428 --> 00:50:58.260
But the setup and then
the getting it all right

00:50:58.260 --> 00:51:00.280
and then the meeting
before and the doing it

00:51:00.280 --> 00:51:01.720
and then the taking the thumbnails

00:51:01.720 --> 00:51:03.550
and then uploading the footage...

00:51:03.550 --> 00:51:06.000
Before you know it you've taken all day

00:51:06.000 --> 00:51:07.740
to shoot two episodes of GMM.

00:51:07.740 --> 00:51:09.689
And that is not me time.

00:51:09.689 --> 00:51:11.380
Right, exactly,

00:51:11.380 --> 00:51:13.740
but I had a little window in between

00:51:16.390 --> 00:51:17.923
the other day and I said,

00:51:19.037 --> 00:51:20.400
"I'm gonna take a walk by myself."

00:51:20.400 --> 00:51:21.233
It was sunny outside.

00:51:21.233 --> 00:51:22.920
I was like, "I'm gonna
take a walk by myself."

00:51:22.920 --> 00:51:24.380
And then Jessie said, "Okay."

00:51:24.380 --> 00:51:26.537
And then she told me this morning,

00:51:26.537 --> 00:51:28.124
"When you said that,"

00:51:28.124 --> 00:51:29.170
because we were talking about this dynamic

00:51:29.170 --> 00:51:30.003
before we recorded this,

00:51:30.003 --> 00:51:31.937
she said, "You know, my instinct is

00:51:31.937 --> 00:51:34.420
"is to be like I wanna
go on a walk with you."

00:51:34.420 --> 00:51:35.253
Like, "If you're gonna go on a walk

00:51:35.253 --> 00:51:36.170
"let's go together."

00:51:36.170 --> 00:51:37.927
But then she's like, "No,
I stopped and realized

00:51:37.927 --> 00:51:41.887
"that no, that's what he needs,

00:51:41.887 --> 00:51:42.900
"that's good for him."

00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:45.560
And it's not saying anything about me.

00:51:45.560 --> 00:51:47.970
Like she was like that's not
saying anything about her

00:51:47.970 --> 00:51:49.880
that I need to get away from her,

00:51:49.880 --> 00:51:51.770
it's saying more about me.

00:51:51.770 --> 00:51:53.740
Yeah, it reminds me of the same principle,

00:51:53.740 --> 00:51:57.543
but Christy made a comment to me

00:51:57.543 --> 00:51:59.890
at the end of the night last night,

00:51:59.890 --> 00:52:01.620
after we had gotten through Survivor

00:52:01.620 --> 00:52:03.453
and then I think there was,

00:52:04.550 --> 00:52:06.840
there was still...

00:52:06.840 --> 00:52:09.840
I said something sarcastic
while everyone was going

00:52:09.840 --> 00:52:13.903
off to bed and she said,

00:52:15.067 --> 00:52:16.617
"You know what, I think the only person

00:52:16.617 --> 00:52:18.647
"that doesn't annoy you is you."

00:52:20.581 --> 00:52:22.010
And then she turned to the whole family

00:52:22.010 --> 00:52:23.857
she's like, "You know
what, I think your dad

00:52:23.857 --> 00:52:26.703
"might need to camp in the backyard,

00:52:27.887 --> 00:52:31.527
"he might need to do a little camping time

00:52:31.527 --> 00:52:33.609
"by himself."

00:52:33.609 --> 00:52:35.970
And I'm like, all right, first of all,

00:52:35.970 --> 00:52:37.050
all right tit for tat,

00:52:37.050 --> 00:52:40.140
I was sarcastic, so message received

00:52:40.140 --> 00:52:43.388
and I laughed and it broke the tension.

00:52:43.388 --> 00:52:46.020
It didn't escalate it, de-escalate,

00:52:46.020 --> 00:52:49.990
she was doing that to
de-escalate and it worked.

00:52:49.990 --> 00:52:54.990
But yeah, I think that
was her observation,

00:52:56.440 --> 00:52:57.760
we're coming to grips with that too

00:52:57.760 --> 00:53:01.440
that I've been getting up an hour

00:53:01.440 --> 00:53:03.390
or an hour and a half
before everybody else

00:53:03.390 --> 00:53:06.275
and I've been meditating.

00:53:06.275 --> 00:53:09.983
Today was 30 days in a row I've meditated.

00:53:10.920 --> 00:53:11.753
Wow.

00:53:11.753 --> 00:53:13.098
Yeah, so it's like,

00:53:13.098 --> 00:53:17.010
it keeps a record of it on the app,

00:53:17.010 --> 00:53:18.720
so it motivates me to do it, like I said.

00:53:18.720 --> 00:53:22.123
I don't know, it's not enough.

00:53:22.123 --> 00:53:22.956
I don't know,

00:53:22.956 --> 00:53:25.390
it's like I still suck
at meditating by the way,

00:53:25.390 --> 00:53:26.560
so I'm not bragging.

00:53:26.560 --> 00:53:30.655
It's just like I am
getting insights, but...

00:53:30.655 --> 00:53:35.220
Insights into myself and how to cope,

00:53:35.220 --> 00:53:38.610
but I'm not quite engaging
those lessons enough,

00:53:38.610 --> 00:53:42.110
but the other thing I
haven't mentioned yet

00:53:42.110 --> 00:53:45.250
as far as relating to Christy goes,

00:53:45.250 --> 00:53:47.280
I think it's been a real
challenge and you know this,

00:53:47.280 --> 00:53:50.653
that Christy's been
sick for over two weeks.

00:53:51.760 --> 00:53:56.760
And it was a mild fever
everyday for the past

00:54:00.750 --> 00:54:04.420
over two weeks that hasn't broken 100,

00:54:04.420 --> 00:54:06.750
but when it's like that
for days and days and days,

00:54:06.750 --> 00:54:10.510
and weeks, she's been in
conversation with her doctor

00:54:12.010 --> 00:54:15.113
and there's been stomach issues,

00:54:16.686 --> 00:54:19.340
so loss of appetite.

00:54:19.340 --> 00:54:21.710
So she's lost over 10 pounds

00:54:21.710 --> 00:54:23.460
because she hasn't been able to eat

00:54:24.678 --> 00:54:26.143
with diarrhea.

00:54:27.528 --> 00:54:29.610
But those are the only symptoms,

00:54:29.610 --> 00:54:31.285
so the doctor was saying well that's,

00:54:31.285 --> 00:54:36.130
COVID was weird and it
manifests in different ways

00:54:36.130 --> 00:54:36.963
in different people,

00:54:36.963 --> 00:54:37.880
but they didn't think that's what it was,

00:54:37.880 --> 00:54:41.390
but then finally after a week and a half,

00:54:41.390 --> 00:54:42.893
I took her to get the test,

00:54:43.750 --> 00:54:45.040
like a drive though test

00:54:45.960 --> 00:54:50.960
and I gotta say, I got a thrill
out of her taking the test

00:54:53.620 --> 00:54:58.253
because it's like a pipe cleaner,

00:55:00.393 --> 00:55:02.210
it's like a freaking pipe cleaner

00:55:02.210 --> 00:55:05.170
that's like that long.

00:55:05.170 --> 00:55:08.550
And they said you'll drive up

00:55:08.550 --> 00:55:11.120
and you can stay in your car
and we'll do the throat swab

00:55:11.970 --> 00:55:14.990
and so Christy was thinking
it was a throat swab,

00:55:14.990 --> 00:55:18.310
but the way that they access
the throat is through the nose.

00:55:18.310 --> 00:55:20.800
Oh gosh.
And so I knew this,

00:55:20.800 --> 00:55:23.110
she didn't know it, so...

00:55:23.110 --> 00:55:25.240
and I didn't know for
sure she didn't know it

00:55:25.240 --> 00:55:26.690
until I saw the look on her face

00:55:26.690 --> 00:55:28.120
as it started to happen.

00:55:28.120 --> 00:55:29.537
The dude reaches in,
he's like, "Now I'm gonna

00:55:29.537 --> 00:55:30.737
"count five seconds.

00:55:30.737 --> 00:55:32.957
"It's gonna feel a lot
longer than five seconds,

00:55:32.957 --> 00:55:34.640
"but I'm just gonna count."

00:55:34.640 --> 00:55:38.440
And then the guy took the pipe cleaner,

00:55:38.440 --> 00:55:39.610
put it in her nose.

00:55:39.610 --> 00:55:41.967
Her eyes got huge, her feet started

00:55:43.400 --> 00:55:45.950
beating the floor board of the car

00:55:45.950 --> 00:55:47.570
and she said that it went all the,

00:55:47.570 --> 00:55:50.133
she said, "If I would have opened my mouth

00:55:50.133 --> 00:55:51.987
"and looked at you,

00:55:51.987 --> 00:55:55.137
"you would've seen the
thing go past my throat,

00:55:55.137 --> 00:55:56.370
"down there and start..."

00:55:56.370 --> 00:55:59.693
And then he's counting to
five and he's twisting it.

00:56:01.920 --> 00:56:03.450
Yeah.

00:56:03.450 --> 00:56:04.840
And then he's pulling it out and...

00:56:06.285 --> 00:56:11.285
I'm sorry, I got a kick out
of her being so surprised

00:56:11.530 --> 00:56:13.591
not because it hurt because it was,

00:56:13.591 --> 00:56:15.290
I wouldn't say it hurt,

00:56:15.290 --> 00:56:17.397
it did hurt, but it
was more uncomfortable.

00:56:17.397 --> 00:56:18.347
It's like when...

00:56:19.322 --> 00:56:21.810
You've experienced it,
we've both experienced it

00:56:21.810 --> 00:56:25.650
because when we went
to the plastic surgeon

00:56:25.650 --> 00:56:27.710
to do the commercial and he showed you

00:56:27.710 --> 00:56:29.010
how to look down at your throat,

00:56:29.010 --> 00:56:30.230
well they did the same thing to me

00:56:30.230 --> 00:56:32.220
at EMT when I was having
some throat issues

00:56:32.220 --> 00:56:33.053
a couple years ago.

00:56:33.053 --> 00:56:34.770
And again, it goes through the nose.

00:56:34.770 --> 00:56:37.523
And I have a deviated
septum and my right nostril

00:56:37.523 --> 00:56:39.111
you can't get in there

00:56:39.111 --> 00:56:42.740
and the left nostril is
open, but prone to bleeding,

00:56:42.740 --> 00:56:47.740
so I just, I don't wanna
have to get tested.

00:56:48.100 --> 00:56:50.063
I hope they come out with...

00:56:50.063 --> 00:56:51.370
I want one of the less invasive ones.

00:56:51.370 --> 00:56:53.440
Two days later we got the results back,

00:56:53.440 --> 00:56:54.473
they were negative.

00:56:55.450 --> 00:56:58.900
She was still having the symptoms and

00:57:00.330 --> 00:57:03.863
I mean, she started to
develop pain in her chest,

00:57:07.350 --> 00:57:11.270
like it was like I think
I'm having trouble breathing

00:57:12.460 --> 00:57:17.460
and I was...

00:57:18.140 --> 00:57:22.480
She had had that similar
symptom earlier in the year

00:57:24.450 --> 00:57:25.950
and she got checked out for that

00:57:25.950 --> 00:57:29.150
and we thought it might be
a heart thing or something.

00:57:29.150 --> 00:57:30.490
She got all checked out and she was fine.

00:57:30.490 --> 00:57:33.320
We realized that anxiety was driving

00:57:33.320 --> 00:57:35.550
a lot of her symptoms at the time

00:57:35.550 --> 00:57:38.330
and then I was making
the point that those,

00:57:38.330 --> 00:57:41.180
I think anxiety might be
manifesting this symptom now

00:57:41.180 --> 00:57:42.750
because even though
your test was negative,

00:57:42.750 --> 00:57:45.720
the doctor did happen to
mention that it's possible

00:57:45.720 --> 00:57:46.990
to have a false negative.

00:57:46.990 --> 00:57:49.570
That she could have COVID, but that...

00:57:49.570 --> 00:57:52.320
And they said if you start
to have breathing problems,

00:57:52.320 --> 00:57:54.240
you need to call us and then we might need

00:57:54.240 --> 00:57:56.240
to take decisive action.

00:57:56.240 --> 00:57:59.140
And then I observed that she started to

00:58:00.820 --> 00:58:02.900
say that she was having
breathing problems.

00:58:02.900 --> 00:58:04.820
She woke up in the middle of the night.

00:58:04.820 --> 00:58:09.210
I woke up three nights ago

00:58:09.210 --> 00:58:13.490
at like 3:30 in the
morning and she was having

00:58:13.490 --> 00:58:15.300
a panic attack,

00:58:15.300 --> 00:58:17.750
but she didn't know if
she just couldn't breathe.

00:58:18.620 --> 00:58:23.510
And it was, I don't, I mean

00:58:23.510 --> 00:58:24.450
I was kinda groggy,

00:58:24.450 --> 00:58:26.910
I'm not the most supportive at 4 AM,

00:58:26.910 --> 00:58:29.980
I was trying to be supportive,

00:58:29.980 --> 00:58:34.980
but I mean ultimately yesterday

00:58:35.260 --> 00:58:39.760
she went in for a lung x-ray

00:58:39.760 --> 00:58:43.082
just so the doctor could
make sure she didn't have it.

00:58:43.082 --> 00:58:44.450
We didn't go to the
emergency room that night,

00:58:44.450 --> 00:58:47.140
I kinda talked her down
and helped her calm down.

00:58:47.140 --> 00:58:50.063
But then the next morning, which
was yesterday, she went in.

00:58:50.966 --> 00:58:54.670
The test results for
that were totally clear,

00:58:54.670 --> 00:58:55.950
her lungs are totally fine,

00:58:55.950 --> 00:59:00.315
which is more support that this is,

00:59:00.315 --> 00:59:03.280
I don't know if I would say psychosomatic,

00:59:03.280 --> 00:59:04.860
maybe that is the right term,

00:59:04.860 --> 00:59:09.310
but it's caused by her heightened anxiety

00:59:10.860 --> 00:59:13.760
associated with being in this pandemic

00:59:13.760 --> 00:59:16.765
where, here's the connection for her,

00:59:16.765 --> 00:59:20.300
over three years ago and
she's told this story

00:59:20.300 --> 00:59:23.360
on Science Mike's podcast

00:59:23.360 --> 00:59:26.970
about how she hit her head,
she had the concussion,

00:59:26.970 --> 00:59:29.460
she still suffers from
post-concussion syndrome,

00:59:29.460 --> 00:59:31.100
but the thing that I think
we're coming to grips with

00:59:31.100 --> 00:59:35.623
is that because of that
she's suffering from PTSD.

00:59:36.780 --> 00:59:39.640
She now on a sub-conscious level,

00:59:39.640 --> 00:59:43.233
believes that if something so,

00:59:44.941 --> 00:59:48.900
if the odds are so low
for her hitting her head

00:59:48.900 --> 00:59:53.030
on a limb walking into the Trader Joe's

00:59:53.030 --> 00:59:56.050
can then three years later have a dramatic

00:59:56.050 --> 00:59:58.360
and drastic impact on her life,

00:59:58.360 --> 01:00:02.693
then well when this virus
starts sweeping through town,

01:00:04.360 --> 01:00:05.433
she's gonna get it.

01:00:06.280 --> 01:00:07.383
She just knows it.

01:00:10.491 --> 01:00:15.143
That's the survival technique
that I think she's learned.

01:00:18.950 --> 01:00:20.867
So it's been difficult.

01:00:21.830 --> 01:00:25.770
With a couple of days of
a flare up of symptoms

01:00:25.770 --> 01:00:27.910
and anxiety, we can work together,

01:00:27.910 --> 01:00:30.493
but when it's over two weeks,

01:00:30.493 --> 01:00:33.120
it starts to become very difficult

01:00:33.120 --> 01:00:37.424
to be empathetic.

01:00:37.424 --> 01:00:39.230
'Cuz it's frustrating.

01:00:39.230 --> 01:00:43.290
It's really frustrating for
me because I feel like I

01:00:43.290 --> 01:00:46.350
don't have the magic words
to get through to her

01:00:46.350 --> 01:00:49.293
or I don't have the techniques to fix her.

01:00:50.240 --> 01:00:52.639
And she's not asking for that from me.

01:00:52.639 --> 01:00:55.320
She's asking for an open ear

01:00:55.320 --> 01:00:58.640
and she's asking for I
think she wants empathy

01:00:58.640 --> 01:01:00.400
and she just wants me to be there for her

01:01:00.400 --> 01:01:03.680
as she goes through this and be supportive

01:01:03.680 --> 01:01:06.000
because she does have a therapist,

01:01:06.000 --> 01:01:09.755
she does have a brain doctor,
she does have a normal doctor,

01:01:09.755 --> 01:01:13.450
and she has other friends that provide

01:01:13.450 --> 01:01:14.470
a support structure too,

01:01:14.470 --> 01:01:16.860
so it's like she doesn't
need everything from me,

01:01:16.860 --> 01:01:18.710
I don't need to be the silver bullet,

01:01:20.360 --> 01:01:24.490
but everyday it gets
increasingly more difficult,

01:01:24.490 --> 01:01:27.232
especially when I see that her
symptoms are getting better

01:01:27.232 --> 01:01:30.393
except for the anxiety.

01:01:31.440 --> 01:01:32.490
So it's...

01:01:34.240 --> 01:01:36.990
And she gave me permission to
talk about this by the way.

01:01:36.990 --> 01:01:38.700
She's like, "What are you gonna
talk about on Ear Biscuits?"

01:01:38.700 --> 01:01:40.340
And I was like, "We're gonna
talk about relational dynamics.

01:01:40.340 --> 01:01:44.350
She's like, "You can talk
about this because..."

01:01:44.350 --> 01:01:45.873
And I applaud her for it that the things

01:01:45.873 --> 01:01:48.670
that I'm sharing are not
things to be embarrassed about,

01:01:48.670 --> 01:01:51.910
they're actual conditions
that people suffer from.

01:01:51.910 --> 01:01:53.610
Whether it's depression, anxiety,

01:01:53.610 --> 01:01:56.020
post-concussion syndrome,

01:01:56.020 --> 01:01:58.783
all types of silent illnesses,

01:02:00.924 --> 01:02:03.020
I think she encouraged
me to share this story

01:02:03.020 --> 01:02:06.340
because just to remove that stigma,

01:02:06.340 --> 01:02:07.510
but you know--

01:02:07.510 --> 01:02:12.310
And I can speak on behalf of
hypochondriacs everywhere,

01:02:12.310 --> 01:02:14.120
which I think people who would relate to

01:02:14.120 --> 01:02:15.790
and I talked to my dad who,

01:02:15.790 --> 01:02:19.513
my dad is like me and presents
himself as invincible,

01:02:21.950 --> 01:02:25.520
but it's like okay, the very first...

01:02:25.520 --> 01:02:28.860
So I went a long time in my life

01:02:28.860 --> 01:02:32.360
without thinking that, not believing,

01:02:32.360 --> 01:02:35.170
like I'd refuse to believe
that you could feel something

01:02:35.170 --> 01:02:40.020
physical, in your body and it not be real.

01:02:40.020 --> 01:02:42.750
Like, but in the past 10 years,

01:02:42.750 --> 01:02:46.050
I have felt so many specific things;

01:02:46.050 --> 01:02:48.420
injuries, and sicknesses,

01:02:48.420 --> 01:02:52.673
and then it turns out not
to be anything and it's

01:02:52.673 --> 01:02:55.063
because and so with Coronavirus,

01:02:55.900 --> 01:02:57.803
the first week that we were home,

01:02:58.770 --> 01:03:03.770
I swear to you, 'cuz I was
consuming so much news,

01:03:04.280 --> 01:03:08.410
I had pain, legitimate pain in my chest.

01:03:08.410 --> 01:03:09.569
And I kep telling Jessie,

01:03:09.569 --> 01:03:11.090
I'm like, "I think I've got it."

01:03:11.090 --> 01:03:12.820
I was like I don't have
any other symptoms,

01:03:12.820 --> 01:03:16.280
but like when I breathe in
it hurt, my chest hurts.

01:03:16.280 --> 01:03:17.690
I was like literally I can point to it,

01:03:17.690 --> 01:03:20.173
it's hurting right here
and it's hurting right here

01:03:20.173 --> 01:03:21.870
and I was like and it comes and it goes

01:03:21.870 --> 01:03:23.900
and I've had it for a week.

01:03:23.900 --> 01:03:27.417
And then I talked to
my dad and he was like,

01:03:27.417 --> 01:03:29.077
"I've had Coronavirus 20 times

01:03:29.077 --> 01:03:30.425
"since this whole thing started,"

01:03:30.425 --> 01:03:35.425
He was like it's just in our genes

01:03:36.761 --> 01:03:40.320
for it to manifest itself in that way,

01:03:40.320 --> 01:03:44.410
so I mean, it doesn't mean you're crazy,

01:03:44.410 --> 01:03:48.490
I think it's very normal
and you can actually...

01:03:48.490 --> 01:03:50.037
And I've had a couple panic attacks.

01:03:50.037 --> 01:03:51.120
I'm not crazy.

01:03:51.120 --> 01:03:53.915
You're saying it doesn't
mean that my wife is crazy?

01:03:53.915 --> 01:03:54.748
Right.

01:03:54.748 --> 01:03:55.879
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:03:55.879 --> 01:04:00.760
There's a couple of like
and that whole feeling like

01:04:00.760 --> 01:04:02.520
you can't breathe thing and like

01:04:02.520 --> 01:04:04.190
a couple of times in the past few weeks

01:04:04.190 --> 01:04:06.110
I've woken up in the middle of the night

01:04:06.110 --> 01:04:07.060
and I've been like,

01:04:08.717 --> 01:04:12.780
"I think I'm not getting a full breath."

01:04:12.780 --> 01:04:17.180
And the moment that you think that,

01:04:17.180 --> 01:04:19.580
it's very difficult to get out of it,

01:04:19.580 --> 01:04:22.070
it's very difficult to get out of it.

01:04:22.070 --> 01:04:25.860
And again, I'm not telling
people that they should

01:04:25.860 --> 01:04:26.790
ignore their symptoms,

01:04:26.790 --> 01:04:30.540
I do think that for me a lot
of times doing what Christy did

01:04:30.540 --> 01:04:33.590
and like going and getting the x-ray so

01:04:33.590 --> 01:04:35.230
you can be told, definitively,

01:04:35.230 --> 01:04:37.250
sometimes you do need that, you need that.

01:04:37.250 --> 01:04:40.740
Like oh no, there's actually
nothing wrong with you

01:04:40.740 --> 01:04:44.157
because hypochondriacs
like me will keep thinking,

01:04:44.157 --> 01:04:45.527
"Yeah, you're just telling
me there's nothing wrong

01:04:45.527 --> 01:04:47.747
"with me, but in reality there is,

01:04:47.747 --> 01:04:50.524
"and you just don't want
me to think about it.

01:04:50.524 --> 01:04:53.290
And sometimes you do
have to go to that place.

01:04:53.290 --> 01:04:56.560
It can get to a place where you're having

01:04:56.560 --> 01:04:58.010
to have all these things
confirmed all the time,

01:04:58.010 --> 01:04:59.800
but I just say that

01:04:59.800 --> 01:05:02.212
as somebody who hasn't
had nearly the issue,

01:05:02.212 --> 01:05:03.747
the struggle that Christy has had

01:05:03.747 --> 01:05:06.130
and I haven't had the
post-concussion syndrome

01:05:06.130 --> 01:05:07.050
or anything like that,

01:05:07.050 --> 01:05:09.480
but I am a hypochondriac
and I have thought

01:05:09.480 --> 01:05:11.807
that I had Coronavirus
already and I was sure of it.

01:05:11.807 --> 01:05:14.370
It's just so difficult to be empathetic

01:05:14.370 --> 01:05:17.220
because I'm not great at it in general,

01:05:17.220 --> 01:05:22.080
but then, and I think when we were talking

01:05:22.080 --> 01:05:23.100
about our highlights from the year,

01:05:23.100 --> 01:05:26.210
I might've talked about the dynamic of,

01:05:26.210 --> 01:05:27.640
I end up...

01:05:27.640 --> 01:05:28.780
I'm not sure, but

01:05:30.300 --> 01:05:35.300
whenever she says I'm
concerned about this,

01:05:35.370 --> 01:05:37.870
whether it's physical
symptoms related to this

01:05:37.870 --> 01:05:40.540
or it's related to the
kids or something else,

01:05:40.540 --> 01:05:44.800
whenever she expresses
anxiety towards something

01:05:44.800 --> 01:05:48.590
my knee jerk reaction,
without actually hearing her

01:05:48.590 --> 01:05:53.590
is to just minimize or try to
make it disappear entirely,

01:05:56.790 --> 01:06:01.103
like to dismiss her instincts

01:06:01.103 --> 01:06:02.990
or her concerns.

01:06:02.990 --> 01:06:07.990
And I told her it's
like at a certain point

01:06:10.030 --> 01:06:11.250
if something's really concerning,

01:06:11.250 --> 01:06:13.074
she'll be like, "You don't
seem concerned at all."

01:06:13.074 --> 01:06:17.117
One time I was like, "If
you cry wolf too many times

01:06:17.117 --> 01:06:20.720
"then the farmer's not gonna come,"

01:06:20.720 --> 01:06:22.660
or I don't know how the story goes.

01:06:22.660 --> 01:06:25.080
And that was not a sensitive thing to say,

01:06:25.080 --> 01:06:26.490
but it was an honest thing.

01:06:26.490 --> 01:06:31.490
That like it's a struggle
to continue to hear her

01:06:33.490 --> 01:06:38.490
and to empathize with
her when it's so foreign

01:06:39.440 --> 01:06:44.440
to my own experience that I think

01:06:46.533 --> 01:06:50.170
I live with my head in the
clouds sometimes maybe.

01:06:50.170 --> 01:06:52.700
Is I think how she feels about me

01:06:52.700 --> 01:06:56.360
because our experience is
so different in this way

01:06:56.360 --> 01:06:59.550
and it's, so it's...

01:06:59.550 --> 01:07:02.941
But it's very frustrated
to not be able to fix it

01:07:02.941 --> 01:07:06.170
and then whenever I start
to say well that's not

01:07:06.170 --> 01:07:08.210
what I need to do, I
just need to be there,

01:07:08.210 --> 01:07:13.040
then it's hard to access the energy to

01:07:14.890 --> 01:07:18.020
because it's against my instincts

01:07:18.020 --> 01:07:20.770
to interact with her in that way.

01:07:20.770 --> 01:07:23.657
So after over two weeks of that,

01:07:23.657 --> 01:07:27.840
again, that was the last
thing of the past two days

01:07:27.840 --> 01:07:32.683
that was like okay, this is difficult.

01:07:37.111 --> 01:07:41.090
Just to close the loop on it,

01:07:41.090 --> 01:07:43.010
what was the corporate saying?

01:07:43.010 --> 01:07:44.190
Circle back.

01:07:44.190 --> 01:07:45.950
I don't wanna have to
circle back on this later,

01:07:45.950 --> 01:07:47.786
so let's close the loop now.

01:07:47.786 --> 01:07:51.560
This morning, I took my meditative time

01:07:51.560 --> 01:07:56.534
to think more about her and about just

01:07:56.534 --> 01:07:59.630
to have an exercise in empathy

01:08:00.670 --> 01:08:03.120
and then afterward, it
gave me the capacity

01:08:03.120 --> 01:08:07.620
to go upstairs and to apologize for

01:08:09.490 --> 01:08:12.710
how I wore my frustration on my sleeve

01:08:12.710 --> 01:08:17.450
and there was no yelling with Christy,

01:08:17.450 --> 01:08:19.678
it wasn't like that.

01:08:19.678 --> 01:08:24.678
It was more of like a just you
could sense the frustration

01:08:25.750 --> 01:08:28.593
and it seemed like annoyance.

01:08:30.228 --> 01:08:33.260
But I think we've had a good
conversation this morning

01:08:33.260 --> 01:08:34.670
and clear the air about that

01:08:34.670 --> 01:08:38.760
and I know that she is
pulling on resources

01:08:38.760 --> 01:08:40.623
and seeing ways that she can,

01:08:44.850 --> 01:08:47.850
there's additional
treatments and therapies

01:08:47.850 --> 01:08:50.110
and things like that will
help her through this.

01:08:50.110 --> 01:08:54.710
That it's not and I can
be there to help her wade

01:08:54.710 --> 01:08:56.520
through that stuff,
but I mean I know she's

01:08:56.520 --> 01:08:59.780
in that process too, so we're not stuck

01:08:59.780 --> 01:09:00.880
and we're not...

01:09:02.788 --> 01:09:04.130
And so we had a moment.

01:09:04.130 --> 01:09:05.340
We had a good moment this morning

01:09:05.340 --> 01:09:07.200
where it was like a reconnection

01:09:07.200 --> 01:09:08.973
and just being honest,

01:09:11.781 --> 01:09:14.620
so that's just to close
the loop on that but...

01:09:17.190 --> 01:09:18.390
Given the...

01:09:19.260 --> 01:09:23.650
There's a lot of things about
this particular situation

01:09:25.520 --> 01:09:28.190
and the things that we continue to read

01:09:28.190 --> 01:09:31.973
about the virus that make it

01:09:31.973 --> 01:09:36.960
a very specifically difficult situation

01:09:36.960 --> 01:09:39.910
for people who are hypochondriacs

01:09:39.910 --> 01:09:44.760
because the symptoms are,

01:09:44.760 --> 01:09:46.310
they don't know anything about it.

01:09:46.310 --> 01:09:50.440
It's like you read one
article and it's like

01:09:50.440 --> 01:09:52.440
okay, well it seems that
it presents this way

01:09:52.440 --> 01:09:55.500
in most people, but and for
some people it's just like this

01:09:55.500 --> 01:09:57.080
and some people all the sudden they go...

01:09:57.080 --> 01:09:58.610
There's all these little things

01:09:58.610 --> 01:10:00.220
that I think people who
write these articles

01:10:00.220 --> 01:10:02.480
and again, they're not, you don't,

01:10:02.480 --> 01:10:03.660
when you're writing about it,

01:10:03.660 --> 01:10:05.950
you don't have hypochondriacs in mind

01:10:05.950 --> 01:10:08.140
and actually hypochondriacs need to learn

01:10:08.140 --> 01:10:11.090
how to deal with, I say
this as a hypochondriac,

01:10:11.090 --> 01:10:13.930
I need to be able to
engage with information

01:10:13.930 --> 01:10:16.850
in a way that doesn't
immediately make me think

01:10:16.850 --> 01:10:17.713
that I have it.

01:10:19.190 --> 01:10:21.410
But it's very difficult
because it manifests itself

01:10:21.410 --> 01:10:22.550
in so many different ways

01:10:22.550 --> 01:10:25.150
and we've been told it's super contagious

01:10:25.150 --> 01:10:27.850
and you're out of your normal routine,

01:10:27.850 --> 01:10:28.730
you gotta...

01:10:28.730 --> 01:10:33.630
All a lot of us are doing
is just reading the news

01:10:33.630 --> 01:10:35.780
and sitting around and thinking.

01:10:35.780 --> 01:10:39.090
And it's like that could be
a really bad combination.

01:10:39.090 --> 01:10:42.367
So I do think it's important that,

01:10:42.367 --> 01:10:44.690
and again, for the longest time

01:10:44.690 --> 01:10:48.790
I just thought that my
mom was a hypochondriac

01:10:48.790 --> 01:10:51.220
and I was like well that's not me

01:10:51.220 --> 01:10:54.457
and then I realized, no, you know what?

01:10:54.457 --> 01:10:55.820
Actually, I am.

01:10:55.820 --> 01:10:58.500
And now talking to my dad,
oh and also my dad is.

01:10:58.500 --> 01:11:02.490
It's like we all have this tendency

01:11:02.490 --> 01:11:04.420
to immediately internalize it,

01:11:04.420 --> 01:11:09.420
so I just think that it's okay.

01:11:09.739 --> 01:11:11.590
It doesn't make you abnormal.

01:11:11.590 --> 01:11:13.850
It's such a common thing,

01:11:13.850 --> 01:11:18.850
but it is important to
realize it and address it.

01:11:19.500 --> 01:11:22.360
I mean, with me, I got a
therapist I can talk to

01:11:22.360 --> 01:11:23.350
about it.

01:11:23.350 --> 01:11:26.260
I think that that continues
to be really important

01:11:26.260 --> 01:11:27.350
for a lot of people.

01:11:27.350 --> 01:11:32.240
There are resources, there's
mental health resources

01:11:32.240 --> 01:11:35.490
that are available in
this very difficult time

01:11:35.490 --> 01:11:37.050
for a lot of people and
we just encourage you

01:11:37.050 --> 01:11:39.690
to continue to seek those out.

01:11:39.690 --> 01:11:40.570
Not a sponsor.

01:11:40.570 --> 01:11:41.980
They were a sponsor at one time,

01:11:41.980 --> 01:11:43.910
but I know there's better help,

01:11:43.910 --> 01:11:46.070
which is like you basically
can have a teleconference

01:11:46.070 --> 01:11:48.040
with somebody, which
is what we're all doing

01:11:48.040 --> 01:11:50.521
with our therapists right now anyway,

01:11:50.521 --> 01:11:53.120
but there are services like that,

01:11:53.120 --> 01:11:56.440
we just encourage you to, if you feel like

01:11:56.440 --> 01:11:58.620
you need somebody to talk
to and don't have somebody

01:11:58.620 --> 01:11:59.733
that you can talk to,

01:12:01.190 --> 01:12:03.430
who will listen and be helpful

01:12:03.430 --> 01:12:05.010
and again, like Link's talking about,

01:12:05.010 --> 01:12:09.030
it's like none of us
are trained in this and

01:12:09.030 --> 01:12:10.280
a lot of times don't know what to say

01:12:10.280 --> 01:12:11.893
and will say the wrong thing.

01:12:12.930 --> 01:12:14.680
Finding somebody who can
actually help you think

01:12:14.680 --> 01:12:16.280
through this and work
through this in a healthy way

01:12:16.280 --> 01:12:17.113
is important.

01:12:18.079 --> 01:12:21.393
Yeah.

01:12:23.195 --> 01:12:25.880
It's helpful to talk about it here.

01:12:25.880 --> 01:12:28.520
I think it's, with Christy's permission,

01:12:28.520 --> 01:12:29.803
it's calculated to talk about it here

01:12:29.803 --> 01:12:32.090
because I know a lot of
people, like you said,

01:12:32.090 --> 01:12:35.800
relate to it and yeah.

01:12:35.800 --> 01:12:39.380
We're not in the business
of providing answers,

01:12:39.380 --> 01:12:41.820
but we're in the business
of just being honest

01:12:41.820 --> 01:12:44.833
and sharing our journey,

01:12:45.894 --> 01:12:49.160
and it's helpful to me to do that,

01:12:49.160 --> 01:12:54.160
so I appreciate opening the can of listen,

01:12:55.760 --> 01:12:56.593
it's your wreck.

01:12:57.850 --> 01:13:02.590
Yeah, so I'm gonna wreck
a book that I listen to

01:13:03.720 --> 01:13:06.966
during my workouts and my strolls.

01:13:06.966 --> 01:13:07.799
Okay.

01:13:07.799 --> 01:13:12.200
And it's called Underland
by Robert MacFarlane.

01:13:12.200 --> 01:13:15.430
And I didn't know about
this Robert MacFarlane guy,

01:13:15.430 --> 01:13:20.430
I didn't know he's a writer from the UK

01:13:20.740 --> 01:13:25.373
who sort of specializes
in writing about nature.

01:13:26.470 --> 01:13:29.530
So it's nonfiction, but
the way that he writes

01:13:29.530 --> 01:13:32.824
about his experience,
specifically in Underland,

01:13:32.824 --> 01:13:36.574
which I've always been
fascinated with geology

01:13:36.574 --> 01:13:39.270
and also just underground
things in general,

01:13:39.270 --> 01:13:43.060
but this is this incredible
multiyear journey

01:13:44.680 --> 01:13:49.193
where he went underground, basically,

01:13:50.520 --> 01:13:54.800
into like caves and mines
and underground systems

01:13:54.800 --> 01:13:59.410
under cities and met the people
who know about these places

01:13:59.410 --> 01:14:00.794
and visited them himself

01:14:00.794 --> 01:14:04.330
and his way of bringing
you along for the ride,

01:14:04.330 --> 01:14:07.554
but it also becomes a
story about the earth

01:14:07.554 --> 01:14:11.093
and the environment and
what's happening in the world.

01:14:13.640 --> 01:14:15.340
Top 10 favorite books of all time.

01:14:15.340 --> 01:14:18.116
What and it's not even, it's nonfiction?

01:14:18.116 --> 01:14:19.750
Yeah.

01:14:19.750 --> 01:14:23.720
And again this isn't for everybody,

01:14:23.720 --> 01:14:26.460
but if you happen to be
interested in the way

01:14:26.460 --> 01:14:28.760
that I described that, if
that piques your interest

01:14:28.760 --> 01:14:32.530
at all, there's nobody that I'm aware of

01:14:32.530 --> 01:14:35.270
who can write in a more compelling way.

01:14:35.270 --> 01:14:37.680
Almost a spiritual way.

01:14:37.680 --> 01:14:40.318
There's like a spiritual experience

01:14:40.318 --> 01:14:43.590
in the way that he approaches this stuff

01:14:43.590 --> 01:14:46.640
that it's again, it's
been super meditative

01:14:46.640 --> 01:14:50.010
to walk and listen to this,

01:14:50.010 --> 01:14:52.140
these stories about these people

01:14:54.240 --> 01:14:58.610
and it's sort of like really tapped

01:14:58.610 --> 01:14:59.890
into my sense of adventure,

01:14:59.890 --> 01:15:02.120
which I feel like I haven't been feeding

01:15:02.120 --> 01:15:03.450
for a long time.

01:15:03.450 --> 01:15:04.770
We do a lot of crazy things,

01:15:04.770 --> 01:15:05.818
our lives are pretty...

01:15:05.818 --> 01:15:07.630
We could do a lot of amazing things,

01:15:07.630 --> 01:15:10.390
but just this guy's commitment

01:15:10.390 --> 01:15:12.860
to going out into the world

01:15:12.860 --> 01:15:15.690
and then talking about
it in a compelling way.

